WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.030
My name is Sami. I'm a restorative dentist

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.070
located in Southern California

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.380
and the owner of a
dental-educational channel

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.360
called Smile Influencers.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.760
One tooth has a broken front tooth,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.000
another tooth has a cavity
underneath an old crown,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.180
and the third tooth has a broken filling,

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.833
and they all need to be fixed.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.833
This case shows a broken
tooth on a mannequin.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.670
Here, I'm using a thin diamond burr

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.430
that I use to create a 45-degree bevel

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.320
onto the tooth's surface.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.230
The white stuff you
see here is Teflon tape

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.390
that I apply to the adjacent teeth

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.910
in order to protect the adjacent teeth

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.190
from the chemicals that I'm about to use.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.490
The blue stuff that I'm using
is phosphoric acid etchant,

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.370
which essentially prepares
the tooth for bonding.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.400
I make sure to wash off
the etchant thoroughly

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.500
in order to decrease the likelihood

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.630
of postoperative sensitivity.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.840
The clear stuff that I'm
applying is a bonding agent

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.600
that I apply to the prepared tooth surface

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.400
and a little bit beyond.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.820
I am curing the tooth
with an LED curing light,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.980
which essentially polymerizes

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.710
any light-cured resin-based composites.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.720
The blue putty that you see here

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.080
is a hydrophilic VPS impression material.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.110
It is a guide that helps us in many cases.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.930
Here, you can see us using composite resin

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.880
packed in incremental layers

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.490
to replace that missing tooth structure

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:39.510
and hopefully create a
filling that is very strong.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.820
And a lot of these have different colors

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.430
that allow us to pick a shade

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.640
that closely matches our patient's teeth.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.650
We also have different composite resins

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.080
that have different
translucency properties,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.820
different opaque properties,

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.220
and this allows us to create a filling

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.040
that looks as natural as possible.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.160
In this particular case,
since it's on a typodont,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.040
we're just using one shade
to give you an example

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.533
of how to build up that
missing tooth structure.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.070
After, I use a series of disks
that go from coarse to fine

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:17.190
to polish the filling, to
make sure there's no ledges,

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.653
and make sure that it's
as smooth as possible.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.300
Now, when patients break their teeth,

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.700
it can be due to a variety of causes.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.980
Trauma. Let's say you get into a fight

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.450
and someone punches you.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.990
Falling down onto a hard
surface like concrete.

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:38.280
Anytime you put a strong
force onto your front teeth,

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.533
you can put it in jeopardy.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.870
This tooth has a crown with an access hole

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:49.950
in the middle of it.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.320
And there is a large
cavity that's developing

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.030
underneath that hole.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.350
Crowns are very strong,

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.510
and they're typically
bonded onto the tooth.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.510
And one of the safest
ways to remove a crown

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.660
is by using a little bit of leverage

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.635
to take the crown off.

00:03:04.635 --> 00:03:07.350
I use a variety of large diamond burrs

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:11.250
in order to clean the
majority of the tooth cavity.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.850
And at the very end, I
use a round carbide burr

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:17.250
that runs at a slower speed

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.433
in order to get the very
last bits of the cavity.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.990
Bonding agent is then used.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.499
We use a microbrush to apply

00:03:33.499 --> 00:03:35.550
the bonding agent onto the tooth.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.700
Core Paste is a composite resin system

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.160
that has a high compressive strength,

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.493
and it replaces a lot of
the lost tooth structure.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.620
And we use an LED curing light to cure

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.990
the outermost layer of the Core Paste,

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:55.650
although it will also
naturally harden over time.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.680
Here we are fabricating an
acrylic temporary crown.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.500
We use temporary cement called TempBond

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.200
to place our temporary
crown onto the tooth.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.720
This essentially maintains the space

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.005
until our lab technician finishes

00:04:09.005 --> 00:04:11.163
fabricating a new dental crown.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:15.690
Typically, cavities form
from poor oral hygiene,

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.090
maybe not brushing or
flossing the right way

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.523
or having a diet that's high in sugar.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.640
This tooth has a broken filling

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.070
with a large cavity developing

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:31.706
between the filling and
their natural tooth.

00:04:31.706 --> 00:04:33.120
In order to clean the decay out,

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.340
I first have to remove
the existing filling.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.430
And in this particular case,
I'm using a diamond burr

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:42.270
to remove all of the old composite resin.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.560
After that's removed, I
use a round carbide burr.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.440
That's used on a slower speed to make sure

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.350
that I remove any infected dentin

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:56.160
or areas where there is a cavity present

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.713
until we have nice
healthy tooth structure.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:05.610
In this particular case,

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.510
I'm using a bonding agent
called Clearfil SE Bond.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.651
The primer and bond essentially

00:05:11.651 --> 00:05:15.377
prepare the tooth surface
for the application

00:05:15.377 --> 00:05:18.030
of our composite resin filling material

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:20.830
in order for the filling material

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.121
to successfully adhere.

00:05:23.121 --> 00:05:24.750
This is called flowable resin.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.210
It's less viscous
compared to other resins,

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.400
but it does a great job at sealing margins

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.160
and preventing postoperative sensitivity.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.220
Packable composite resins
are highly viscous resins

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.620
that contain a high volume
of filler particles,

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.113
which gives them a stiff consistency.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.420
At the very end, we are adjusting
the shape of the filling

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.450
in order to make the
occlusion of the patient

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:50.760
feel as natural as possible.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.070
We're hoping that when our patient moves

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.020
left and right, front and back

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.311
that there is no interference

00:05:57.311 --> 00:05:59.436
between the patient's motions

00:05:59.436 --> 00:06:01.893
and the new filling that's in place.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:04.440
I always tell my patients
that there are things

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.390
that you can do to protect your teeth,

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.940
like brushing and flossing
in the morning and at night.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:10.350
Avoid dangerous habits

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:13.140
like opening packages with your teeth,

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.240
because although our
teeth are very strong,

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.300
they can break, and we
want to be very careful

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.093
and conscientious about
what we put in our mouth.

